\title{The Prime-Power Excess:\\ An Exact Theory of the Radical-to-Full Feature Lift}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete deterministic account of a phenomenon observed empirically in
multiplicative-feature regression: when a \emph{prime-power} feature is added on top of a
\emph{radical} (distinct-prime) feature, the coefficient of determination rises by a small but
strikingly reproducible amount, and the rise grows with the length of the integer window used.
Writing $\rad n=\prod_{p\mid n}p$, the entire information gap between the two features is the
\emph{prime-power excess} $E(n)=\log n-\log(\rad n)=\sum_{p\mid n}(v_p(n)-1)\log p$.
We prove: (i) $E\ge 0$ with equality exactly on squarefree integers, and $E$ is not a function of
$\rad$, with an explicit irreducible-residual bound $\tfrac12(E(m)-E(n))^2$ at every radical
collision; (ii) an exact arithmetic identity $E(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\Lsharp(d)$, where $\Lsharp$ is the
von Mangoldt function restricted to non-prime prime powers, whence the exact window law
$\sum_{n\le N}E(n)=\sum_{d\le N}\Lsharp(d)\lfloor N/d\rfloor$ and a linear density floor with
constant tending to $\sum_p\log p/(p(p-1))\approx 0.7554$; (iii) a seed-uniform window law
$|\mathcal{M}(a,w)-w\rho(M)|\le\Psi(M)$ with offset-free and window-length-free error, giving cross-offset
dispersion $\le 2\Psi(M)$ against a main term linear in $w$, and
$\#\operatorname{supp}\Lsharp\cap[1,M]\le\sqrt M(\log_2 M+1)$; (iv) a \emph{fibrewise variance law}:
for any finite design, the optimal radical-only predictor has residual exactly the within-fibre sum
of squares, so the lift over the best base model equals \emph{exactly} $W/T$, the fraction of target
variance interior to the radical fibres --- strictly positive iff the design contains a radical
collision with differing prime-power content, and zero otherwise; (v) a smooth-pool floor
$E(n)\ge\log n-y\log 4$ for $y$-smooth $n$, forcing $E(n)>0$ for all $y$-smooth $n>4^y$ and yielding
$\Delta R^2=1$ on the $2$-smooth tower, where the total sum of squares is
$\frac{m(m^2-1)}{12}(\log 2)^2$; and (vi) a graded filtration
$F_j(n)=\sum_p\min(v_p(n),j)\log p$ whose layers decay geometrically,
$\sum_{n\le N}L_{k+1}(n)\le\frac12\sum_{n\le N}L_k(n)$, so that the square layer sandwiches the whole
effect within a factor $2$. Explicit worked instances include $\Delta R^2=3/4$ on the design
$\{2,3,4\}$ and the unconditional bound $\sum_{n\le N}E(n)\ge N/4-2$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} prime-power excess, radical, von Mangoldt function, Chebyshev identity,
variance decomposition, coefficient of determination, smooth numbers, Dirichlet hyperbola swap.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical phenomenon}

Consider a regression problem on a window of integers in which the response is a multiplicative
statistic and the available predictors are built from the prime factorisation. Two natural features
present themselves: the \emph{base (radical) feature} $B(n)=\sum_{p\mid n}\log p=\log(\rad n)$,
recording \emph{which} primes divide $n$; and the \emph{prime-power feature}
$P(n)=\sum_p v_p(n)\log p=\log n$, recording additionally \emph{how often}.

A multi-seed experiment fitting on windows of consecutive integers drawn from smooth-number pools
reported the following. At smoothness parameter $u=3.5$ and window length $w=240$, the increment in
coefficient of determination obtained by adding the prime-power feature was
$+0.055,\ +0.049,\ +0.051,\ +0.050,\ +0.048$
across five independent seeds: all five above $0.03$, cross-seed standard deviation $0.0025$,
standard error of the mean $0.0011$, with confidence intervals excluding zero in all five cells at
both smoothness settings. Moreover the mean lift \emph{increased} with window length: from $0.051$
to $0.058$ at $u=3.5$ and from $0.058$ to $0.082$ at $u=2.5$ as $w$ went from $240$ to $960$.

Three qualitative facts thus demand explanation: the lift is (a) positive, (b) reproducible across
seeds with dispersion an order of magnitude below the effect, and (c) monotone increasing in window
length, more so for smoother pools.

\subsection{Thesis}

All three facts are \emph{arithmetic}, not statistical. Every one of them follows from deterministic
properties of the difference of the two features, and the results below hold for the \emph{best
possible} predictor in the base class, however that class is realised.

\begin{definition}[Prime-power excess]
For $n\ge 1$, $\; E(n)=\log n-\log(\rad n)$, where $\rad n=\prod_{p\mid n}p$.
\end{definition}

Throughout, $p$ denotes a prime, $v_p(n)$ the exponent of $p$ in $n$, $\Lambda$ the von Mangoldt
function ($\Lambda(p^k)=\log p$ for $k\ge1$, else $0$), and $\lfloor\cdot\rfloor$ integer division.
All logarithms are natural.

\section{The lift quantity and the irreducible error of base models}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-power identity]\label{thm:ident}
For every $n\ne 0$, $\; E(n)=\sum_{p\mid n}\bigl(v_p(n)-1\bigr)\log p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unique factorisation gives $\log n=\sum_{p\mid n}v_p(n)\log p$, while the definition of the radical
gives $\log(\rad n)=\sum_{p\mid n}\log p$, both sums over the same index set. Subtract termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-negativity]\label{cor:nonneg}
$E(n)\ge 0$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $p\mid n$ we have $v_p(n)\ge 1$ and $\log p>0$, so every term of Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} is
non-negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Squarefree characterisation]\label{thm:sqfree}
For $n\ne 0$: $E(n)=0$ if and only if $n$ is squarefree.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $n$ is squarefree then $\rad n=n$, so $E(n)=0$. Conversely, a vanishing sum of non-negative terms
has all terms zero; since $\log p>0$ this forces every $v_p(n)=1$, i.e.\ all exponents $\le 1$.
\end{proof}

Thus $E$ is \emph{pure repeated-prime information}: identically zero where the base feature is
already a complete description, and supported exactly where a prime occurs with multiplicity.

\begin{proposition}[Quantitative lower bounds]\label{prop:sqdvd}
If $p^2\mid n$ with $n\ne0$ then $E(n)\ge\log p$; in particular $4\mid n$ implies $E(n)\ge\log 2$.
For a prime power, $E(p^k)=(k-1)\log p$ for $k\ge1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Under $p^2\mid n$ the single term at $p$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} is at least $\log p$ and the
others are non-negative. For prime powers, $\rad(p^k)=p$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Radical collisions force base-model error}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{radical collision} in a design $S$ is a pair $m,n\in S$ with $\rad m=\rad n$ and
$E(m)\ne E(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Irreducible residual at a collision]\label{thm:collision}
Let $\rad m=\rad n$. Then for every $f:\N\to\R$,
\[
\bigl(E(m)-f(\rad m)\bigr)^2+\bigl(E(n)-f(\rad n)\bigr)^2\;\ge\;\tfrac12\bigl(E(m)-E(n)\bigr)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $t=f(\rad m)=f(\rad n)$, $a=E(m)$, $b=E(n)$. Then
$(a-t)^2+(b-t)^2-\tfrac12(a-b)^2=\tfrac12(a+b-2t)^2\ge0$.
\end{proof}

The bound is uniform over the entire class of base-only predictors: linear, polynomial, tabulated,
or arbitrarily expressive.

\begin{corollary}[A strictly positive floor at every prime]
For every prime $p$ and every $f$,
\[
0<\tfrac12(\log p)^2\le\bigl(E(p^2)-f(\rad p^2)\bigr)^2+\bigl(E(p)-f(\rad p)\bigr)^2,
\]
whereas the exact prime-power model has residual $0$ on the same design.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\rad(p^2)=\rad p=p$, $E(p)=0$, $E(p^2)=\log p$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:sqdvd}; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:collision}.
\end{proof}

The smallest instance, $\rad 4=\rad 2=2$ with $E(4)=\log 2\ne 0=E(2)$, already witnesses that $E$ is
not a function of the radical.

\subsection{The offset-uniform window floor}

\begin{definition}[Window mass]
For $a,w\in\N$, $\;\mathcal{M}(a,w)=\sum_{a\le n<a+w}E(n)$.
\end{definition}

$\mathcal{M}$ is non-negative and additive, $\mathcal{M}(a,w+v)=\mathcal{M}(a,w)+\mathcal{M}(a+w,v)$, hence monotone in $w$.

\begin{theorem}[Offset-uniform floor]\label{thm:floor}
For every offset $a\ge1$ and every $w$, $\;\mathcal{M}(a,w)\ge\lfloor w/4\rfloor\log 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $c$ be the least multiple of $4$ that is $\ge a$. The integers $c,c+4,\dots,c+4(\lfloor
w/4\rfloor-1)$ are $\lfloor w/4\rfloor$ distinct multiples of $4$ inside $[a,a+w)$, each
contributing $E\ge\log 2$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:sqdvd}; the remaining terms are non-negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Growth in window length]\label{cor:growth}
For $a\ge1$: $\mathcal{M}(a,w+4)\ge \mathcal{M}(a,w)+\log 2$; $\mathcal{M}(a,w)>0$ once $w\ge4$; $\mathcal{M}(a,240)\ge 60\log2\approx41.59$;
$\mathcal{M}(a,960)\ge240\log2\approx166.36$; and $\mathcal{M}(a,960)\ge \mathcal{M}(a,240)+180\log 2\approx \mathcal{M}(a,240)+124.77$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Additivity plus Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} applied to the appended block, whose floor is offset-free.
\end{proof}

\section{The lift as a von Mangoldt mass: an exact window law}

\begin{definition}[Prime-power weight]
$\Lsharp(d)=0$ if $d$ is prime, and $\Lsharp(d)=\Lambda(d)$ otherwise. Thus $\Lsharp$ is supported
exactly on the prime powers $p^k$ with $k\ge2$, where it equals $\log p$; it is non-negative.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Chebyshev split]\label{thm:cheb}
For every $n$, $\; E(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\Lsharp(d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Chebyshev's identity $\sum_{d\mid n}\Lambda(d)=\log n$ runs over prime-power divisors. Split
according to whether $d$ is prime or a higher prime power: the prime part is
$\sum_{p\mid n}\log p=\log(\rad n)$ and the higher part is $\sum_{d\mid n}\Lsharp(d)$. Subtract.
\end{proof}

In words: \emph{the base feature is precisely the prime part of Chebyshev's identity, and the lift
is precisely the higher-prime-power part.}

\begin{lemma}[Dirichlet swap]\label{lem:swap}
For every $N$ and every $f:\N\to\R$, $\;\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{d\mid n}f(d)=\sum_{d=1}^{N}f(d)\lfloor
N/d\rfloor$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write the divisor sum as $\sum_{d\le N}\mathbf{1}[d\mid n]f(d)$ and interchange; the number of
$n\in[1,N]$ divisible by $d$ is $\lfloor N/d\rfloor$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact window law]\label{thm:window}
For every $N$, $\;\sum_{n\le N}E(n)=\sum_{d\le N}\Lsharp(d)\lfloor N/d\rfloor$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:cheb} with Lemma~\ref{lem:swap}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear density floor]\label{thm:density}
For any finite $D\subseteq[1,N]$,
\[
\sum_{n\le N}E(n)\;\ge\;\Bigl(\sum_{d\in D}\frac{\Lsharp(d)}{d}\Bigr)N-\sum_{d\in D}\Lsharp(d).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
All terms of Theorem~\ref{thm:window} are non-negative, so restricting to $D$ decreases the sum;
then use $\lfloor N/d\rfloor>N/d-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit numerical floor]
For $N\ge8$, $\;\sum_{n\le N}E(n)\ge N/4-2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $D=\{4,8\}$, on which $\Lsharp=\log2$. The density is $\tfrac38\log2\approx0.2599>1/4$ and the
additive error is $2\log 2<2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The true density constant]\label{rem:const}
Letting $D$ exhaust all higher prime powers, the attainable densities increase to
\[
\sum_{k\ge2}\sum_p\frac{\log p}{p^k}=\sum_p\frac{\log p}{p(p-1)}\approx 0.7554 .
\]
Thus $\sum_{n\le N}E(n)=0.7554\ldots N+O(\sqrt N\log^2 N)$: the aggregate lift is linear in the
window length with an explicit slope of roughly $0.755$ nats per additional integer.
\end{remark}

\section{Seed stability: the lift depends on the window, not on the seed}

We model a seed by the \emph{offset} of its window.

\begin{definition}
For a truncation level $M$,
$\rho(M)=\sum_{d\le M}\Lsharp(d)/d$ (prime-power density) and
$\Psi(M)=\sum_{d\le M}\Lsharp(d)$ (total prime-power weight).
\end{definition}

$\rho$ increases to $\approx0.7554$; $\Psi(M)=\psi(M)-\theta(M)$ is a classical Chebyshev difference,
of size $\asymp\sqrt M$.

\begin{theorem}[Seed-uniform window law]\label{thm:seed}
Let $a\ge1$ and $M\ge a-1+w$. Then $\;|\mathcal{M}(a,w)-w\rho(M)|\le\Psi(M)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $A=a-1$, so $\mathcal{M}(a,w)=\mathcal{M}(1,A+w)-\mathcal{M}(1,A)$ by additivity. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:window} to both and
extend each sum to $[1,M]$ (harmless, since $\lfloor N/d\rfloor=0$ for $d>N$), obtaining
\[
\mathcal{M}(a,w)=\sum_{d\le M}\Lsharp(d)\Bigl(\Bigl\lfloor\tfrac{A+w}{d}\Bigr\rfloor-\Bigl\lfloor\tfrac{A}{d}\Bigr\rfloor\Bigr).
\]
For each $d$ the bracket counts multiples of $d$ in an interval of length $w$, hence differs from
$w/d$ by at most $1$. Multiply by $\Lsharp(d)\ge0$ and sum.
\end{proof}

The main term $w\rho(M)$ does not involve $a$; the error $\Psi(M)$ involves neither $a$ nor $w$.

\begin{theorem}[Cross-seed dispersion]\label{thm:disp}
If $a,b\ge1$ and $M$ dominates both windows, then $\;|\mathcal{M}(a,w)-\mathcal{M}(b,w)|\le 2\Psi(M)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Triangle inequality applied to Theorem~\ref{thm:seed} at $a$ and $b$; the common main term cancels.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sparsity of the arithmetic kernel]\label{thm:sparse}
The support of $\Lsharp$ inside $[1,M]$ has cardinality at most $\sqrt M\,(\log_2 M+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every such $d$ is $p^k$ with $k\ge2$; then $p^2\le M$ gives $p\le\sqrt M$ and $2^k\le M$ gives
$k\le\log_2 M$. The map $d\mapsto(p,k)$ is injective by unique factorisation.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Each surviving weight is at most $\log M$, so $\Psi(M)\le\sqrt M\log M(\log_2M+1)$: sublinear, against
a main term linear in $w$. A random-noise explanation would predict cross-seed dispersion growing
like $\sqrt w$; the theory predicts $O(1)$ in $w$, which is what was observed.
\end{remark}

\section{The fibrewise variance law: an exact formula for the lift}

Let $S$ be a finite design, $g$ a base statistic (for us $g=\rad$), $y$ a target (for us $y=E$).

\begin{definition}
The \emph{fibre} of $g$ over $c$ is $S_c=\{n\in S: g(n)=c\}$. For finite $F$, the \emph{total sum of
squares} is $T(F)=\sum_{n\in F}\bigl(y(n)-\bar y_F\bigr)^2$ with $\bar y_F=|F|^{-1}\sum_{j\in F}y(j)$,
and the \emph{within-fibre sum of squares} of the design is $W(S,g,y)=\sum_{c\in g(S)}T(S_c)$. The
coefficient of determination of a predictor $\hat y$ is
$R^2=1-\bigl(\sum_{n\in S}(y(n)-\hat y(n))^2\bigr)/T(S)$. A \emph{base model} is any predictor of the
form $n\mapsto f(g(n))$, with no restriction on $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[The mean minimises squared error]\label{lem:mean}
For finite non-empty $F$ and any $b\in\R$, $\;T(F)\le\sum_{n\in F}(y(n)-b)^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sum_{n\in F}(y(n)-b)^2=\sum y^2-2b\sum y+|F|b^2$ is a convex quadratic in $b$ minimised at
$b=\bar y_F$, where its value is $T(F)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound for base models]\label{thm:lower}
For every $f$, $\;W(S,g,y)\le\sum_{n\in S}\bigl(y(n)-f(g(n))\bigr)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $S$ into fibres. On $S_c$ the predictor is the constant $f(c)$, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:mean}
its contribution is at least $T(S_c)$. Sum over fibres.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment by the fibrewise mean]\label{thm:attain}
Let $\mu(c)=|S_c|^{-1}\sum_{j\in S_c}y(j)$. Then
$\sum_{n\in S}\bigl(y(n)-\mu(g(n))\bigr)^2=W(S,g,y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition into fibres; on $S_c$ the predictor is the constant $\bar y_{S_c}$, contributing exactly
$T(S_c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:bestbase}
If $T(S)>0$ then every base model has $R^2\le1-W/T(S)$, with equality for the fibrewise mean.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Fibrewise variance law]\label{thm:fibre}
Let $T=T(S)>0$. The lift of the exact model $\hat y=y$ over the best base model is
\[
\Delta R^2=R^2(y)-R^2(\mu\circ g)=\frac{W(S,g,y)}{T}.
\]
Against an arbitrary base model $f$ one has $\Delta R^2\ge W/T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$R^2(y)=1$ since the residual vanishes; $R^2(\mu\circ g)=1-W/T$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:bestbase}.
Subtract. The inequality follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} The measured lift is \emph{exactly} the fraction of the target's
variance that lives \emph{inside} the fibres of the base statistic: for $g=\rad$, $y=E$, the fraction
of the variance of prime-power content that survives conditioning on the squarefree skeleton. It is a
purely arithmetic ratio, with no dependence on estimator, optimiser, regulariser or sample size.

\subsection{The collision dichotomy}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tsspos}
If $m,n\in F$ with $y(m)\ne y(n)$ then $T(F)>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T(F)\ge0$; vanishing would force $y$ to equal its mean at every point of $F$, hence to be constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positive lift from one collision]\label{thm:pos}
If $S$ contains $m,n$ with $g(m)=g(n)$ and $y(m)\ne y(n)$, then $W>0$ and hence $\Delta R^2>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both lie in the fibre over $c=g(m)$, so $T(S_c)>0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:tsspos}; all other fibre
variances are $\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero lift without collisions]\label{thm:zero}
If $y$ is constant on every fibre of $g$ in $S$, then $W=0$ and $\Delta R^2=0$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each fibre has constant $y$, so its mean equals that constant and $T(S_c)=0$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:pos} and~\ref{thm:zero} form a sharp dichotomy: \emph{the lift is strictly positive
if and only if the window contains a radical collision with differing prime-power content, and is
exactly zero otherwise.} For the specific pair $g=\rad$, $y=E$ the criterion simplifies
dramatically.

\begin{proposition}[Radical fibres are automatically informative]\label{prop:inj}
If $m\ne n$ and $\rad m=\rad n$, then $E(m)\ne E(n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Once the radicals agree, $E(m)-E(n)=\log m-\log n$, and $\log$ is injective on positive integers.
\end{proof}

Hence the lift is strictly positive if and only if some radical value is attained twice in the
design, and exactly $0$ if all radicals are distinct. Repeated radicals are \emph{not} the same as
non-squarefree entries: a design of distinct squarefree integers has all radicals distinct, hence
zero lift, whatever its size. Among \emph{consecutive} integers repeated radicals are comparatively
scarce, so the lift on such windows is modest; among \emph{smooth} pools --- the setting of the
measurement --- they are unavoidable, by pigeonhole.

\begin{proposition}[Pigeonhole in a smooth pool]\label{prop:pigeon}
Every $y$-smooth integer has $\rad n\mid y\#$, so a pool of $y$-smooth integers realises at most
$2^{\pi(y)}$ distinct radicals. Consequently any pool of more than $2^{\pi(y)}$ distinct $y$-smooth
integers has a repeated radical and hence $\Delta R^2>0$; at least $|S|-2^{\pi(y)}$ of its members
lie in non-singleton fibres.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The radical of a $y$-smooth $n$ is a squarefree divisor of $y\#$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:primorial}), and
$y\#$ has exactly $2^{\pi(y)}$ divisors; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} with Proposition~\ref{prop:inj}.
\end{proof}

The observed $\Delta R^2\approx0.05$ is therefore a measurement of the repeated-radical structure of
the design, weighted by disagreement magnitude --- not a fitting artefact. Numerically, on the pool
of all $y$-smooth integers up to $10^5$ the exact value $W/T$ is $0.935$ at $y=7$, $0.745$ at
$y=13$, $0.493$ at $y=31$ and $0.375$ at $y=97$: monotone decreasing in $y$, precisely the direction
of the observed smoothness dependence.

\subsection{A fully explicit intermediate value}

\begin{theorem}[The design $\{2,3,4\}$]\label{thm:d234}
Let $S=\{2,3,4\}$, $g=\rad$, $y=E$. Then
\[
T(S)=\tfrac23(\log2)^2,\qquad W(S,\rad,E)=\tfrac12(\log2)^2,\qquad \Delta R^2=\tfrac34 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$E(2)=E(3)=0$ (both squarefree) and $E(4)=\log2$; the mean is $\tfrac13\log2$, so
$T=2(\tfrac13\log2)^2+(\tfrac23\log2)^2=\tfrac23(\log2)^2$. The radical fibres are $S_2=\{2,4\}$ and
$S_3=\{3\}$; the singleton contributes $0$, and on $\{2,4\}$ the mean is $\tfrac12\log2$, giving
$T(S_2)=\tfrac12(\log2)^2$. Then $\Delta R^2=W/T=(1/2)/(2/3)=3/4$.
\end{proof}

This is a genuinely intermediate value, strictly between the degenerate extremes $0$ and $1$.

\section{The smooth regime: why smoother pools show a larger lift}

\begin{definition}
$n$ is \emph{$y$-smooth} if every prime factor of $n$ is $\le y$. The primorial is
$y\#=\prod_{p\le y}p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Primorial ceiling]\label{thm:primorial}
If $n$ is $y$-smooth then $\rad n\mid y\#$, hence $\log(\rad n)\le\log(y\#)\le y\log 4$, the last step
by Chebyshev's bound $y\#\le 4^{y}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\rad n$ is a product of distinct primes $\le y$, i.e.\ a sub-product of the squarefree $y\#$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Smooth floor]\label{thm:smoothfloor}
If $n$ is $y$-smooth then $E(n)\ge\log n-\log(y\#)\ge\log n-y\log4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Every $y$-smooth $n>4^y$ has $E(n)>0$; equivalently, no $y$-smooth integer exceeding $4^y$ is
squarefree.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:smoothfloor} gives $E(n)>0$; then apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sqfree}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$u$-form of the floor]
If $n$ is $y$-smooth with $n\ge y^{u}$ and $y\ge1$, then $\;E(n)\ge u\log y-y\log 4$.
\end{theorem}

At a fixed size scale, decreasing $y$ reduces the ceiling $y\log4$ on what the base feature can
explain, so a larger share of $\log n$ must be carried by the prime-power feature: the deterministic
content of the observed smoothness dependence.

\subsection{The extreme case: exact $R^2$ on the $2$-smooth tower}

Let $S_m=\{2,4,8,\dots,2^m\}$, of cardinality $m$. On $S_m$ the base statistic is \emph{constant}
($\rad(2^k)=2$), while the target is $E(2^k)=(k-1)\log2$.

\begin{theorem}[Total variance of the tower]\label{thm:tower}
For $m\ge1$,
\[
\sum_{n\in S_m}E(n)=\frac{m(m-1)}{2}\log 2,\qquad
T(S_m)=\frac{m(m^2-1)}{12}(\log2)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The mass is a triangular number. The mean is $\frac{m-1}{2}\log2$, and
$T=(\log2)^2\bigl(\sum_{j<m}j^2-\frac1m(\sum_{j<m}j)^2\bigr)$; the closed forms
$\sum_{j<m}j=m(m-1)/2$ and $\sum_{j<m}j^2=m(m-1)(2m-1)/6$ give $m(m^2-1)/12$. In particular $T>0$
for $m\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Total collapse of the base class]
For $m\ge2$ and every $f$, the base model $n\mapsto f(\rad n)$ has $R^2\le0$ on $S_m$; the exact
prime-power model has $R^2=1$; hence $\Delta R^2\ge1$, with equality for the best base model.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\rad$ is constant on $S_m$, so every base model is a constant predictor, whose residual is at least
$T(S_m)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:mean}; hence $R^2\le0$. This is the single-fibre case $W=T$ of
Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}.
\end{proof}

\section{The graded hierarchy: diminishing returns of higher-order features}

\begin{definition}[Graded features and layers]
For $j\ge1$,
\[
F_j(n)=\sum_{p\mid n}\min\bigl(v_p(n),j\bigr)\log p,\qquad
L_k(n)=\sum_{p:\,p^k\mid n}\log p .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Filtration]\label{thm:filt}
$F_1(n)=\log(\rad n)$; $F_j(n)=\log n$ once $j\ge\max_p v_p(n)$; and
$F_{j+1}(n)-F_j(n)=L_{j+1}(n)$ for $n\ne0$. Consequently, for any saturating $J$,
$E(n)=\sum_{j=1}^{J-1}L_{j+1}(n)=L_2(n)+L_3(n)+\cdots$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\min(v,j+1)-\min(v,j)$ equals $1$ iff $v\ge j+1$, i.e.\ iff $p^{j+1}\mid n$. Telescoping from $F_1$
to $F_J$ gives $\log n-\log(\rad n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity and finiteness]
$L_k(n)\ge0$; $L_l(n)\le L_k(n)$ for $k\le l$; and $L_k(n)>0$ forces $n\ge2^k$, so at most $\log_2 n$
layers are non-zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Layer window law]\label{thm:layerlaw}
For $k\ge1$, with $\mathcal{L}_k(N)=\sum_{n\le N}L_k(n)$,
\[
\mathcal{L}_k(N)=\sum_{d\le N}\lambda_k(d)\Bigl\lfloor\frac{N}{d}\Bigr\rfloor
=\sum_{p\le N}\log p\,\Bigl\lfloor\frac{N}{p^k}\Bigr\rfloor,
\]
where $\lambda_k$ is supported exactly on the $k$-th powers of primes with value $\log p$ at $p^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$L_k(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\lambda_k(d)$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:swap} and reindex $d=p^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric decay]\label{thm:decay}
For $k\ge1$ and every $N$: $\;\mathcal{L}_{k+1}(N)\le\tfrac12\mathcal{L}_k(N)$, hence
$\mathcal{L}_{k+j}(N)\le2^{-j}\mathcal{L}_k(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Termwise in Theorem~\ref{thm:layerlaw}: for $p\ge2$,
$2\lfloor N/p^{k+1}\rfloor\le p\lfloor N/p^{k+1}\rfloor\le\lfloor N/p^{k}\rfloor$, because
$p\lfloor N/p^{k+1}\rfloor$ is a multiple of $p$ not exceeding $N/p^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Level-$2$ sandwich]
For every $N$: $\;\mathcal{L}_2(N)\le\sum_{n\le N}E(n)\le2\,\mathcal{L}_2(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The left inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:filt} plus non-negativity. For the right,
$\sum_{n\le N}E(n)=\sum_{j\ge0}\mathcal{L}_{2+j}(N)\le\mathcal{L}_2(N)\sum_{j\ge0}2^{-j}$, the sum
being finite since $\mathcal{L}_k(N)=0$ for $N<2^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Diminishing returns]
For $k\ge1$, $\;\sum_{j\ge0}\mathcal{L}_{k+j}(N)\le2\,\mathcal{L}_k(N)$: the cumulative worth of all
features of order above $k$ never exceeds twice the worth of level $k$ itself.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Prime-power excess by sieve.}
Accumulate $\Lsharp$ over multiples: for each prime $p\le\sqrt N$ and each $k\ge2$ with $p^k\le N$,
add $\log p$ to every multiple of $p^k$. Total cost $O(N\log\log N)$, since
$\sum_{p,k\ge2}N/p^k$ converges (Remark~\ref{rem:const}).

\paragraph{Exact window mass.}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:window}, $\sum_{n\le N}E(n)=\sum_{k\ge2,\,p^k\le N}\log p\lfloor N/p^k\rfloor$, a
sum over $O(\sqrt N\log N)$ terms, computable in $\tilde O(\sqrt N)$ time without touching the
individual $E(n)$. For an offset window, difference two such evaluations.

\paragraph{The exact lift by fibrewise variance.}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}, group the design by $\rad$, set $W$ to the sum of within-group sums of
squares of $E$ and $T$ to the total sum of squares; then $\Delta R^2=W/T$. Linear time after
factorisation: no optimiser, no randomness.

\paragraph{Layer profile.}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:layerlaw}, $\mathcal{L}_k(N)=\sum_{p\le N^{1/k}}\log p\lfloor N/p^k\rfloor$;
computing $k=2,\dots,\log_2N$ costs $\tilde O(\sqrt N)$ and exhibits the decay of
Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} directly.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Feature-selection guidance.} Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre} predicts the exact worth of adding
exponent information before any model is trained: zero on collision-free designs, and $W/T$
otherwise, computable in linear time.

\textbf{Diagnostics against overfitting.} A reported lift can be compared with $W/T$. A measured lift
below $W/T$ indicates an under-expressive or under-optimised baseline; a measured lift above $W/T$ is
impossible for a genuine base model and indicates leakage of non-radical information.

\textbf{Window-length planning.} Signal $\approx\rho w$ grows linearly while cross-seed dispersion
$\le2\Psi(M)$ is constant in $w$: to raise a $z$-score, lengthen the window rather than adding seeds
--- exactly the behaviour observed under $w:240\to960$.

\textbf{Smooth-number computations.} The statement that every $y$-smooth $n>4^y$ has a repeated prime
factor certifies that smooth pools above the primorial threshold are automatically rich in
prime-power content.

\textbf{A stopping rule for graded features.} The tail bound $2\mathcal{L}_k(N)$ gives a fixed
relative tolerance after $O(\log(1/\varepsilon))$ levels.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was actually measured}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Observation & Deterministic explanation \\
\midrule
Lift is positive & Radical collisions force residual $\ge\frac12(\Delta E)^2$ \\
Lift is stable across seeds & Offset-free window law; dispersion $\le2\Psi(M)$ \\
Lift grows with window length & Aggregate mass linear in $w$, slope $\to0.7554$ \\
Lift is larger for smoother pools & Base feature capped by $\log(y\#)\le y\log4$ \\
Higher-order features add little & Geometric decay $\mathcal{L}_{k+1}\le\mathcal{L}_k/2$ \\
Exact numerical value of the lift & $\Delta R^2=W/T$, the within-fibre variance fraction \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Sharpness}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre} is an equality and both degenerate cases are realised: $\Delta R^2=0$ on
collision-free designs and $\Delta R^2=1$ on the single-fibre tower; the design $\{2,3,4\}$ realises
the intermediate value $3/4$. The constant $1/2$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} is set by the smallest
prime and is essentially optimal for the argument given.

\subsection{Limitations}

We model the sampling seed by the window offset; the theorems bound all offset-induced variability by
$2\Psi(M)$ but not necessarily every source of cross-seed dispersion. Our exact lift formula compares
the \emph{best} base model with the \emph{exact} prime-power model; empirical fits use restricted
hypothesis classes, for which Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} gives the correct one-sided statement
$\Delta R^2\ge W/T$. Finally, the theory's target is $E$ itself; the fibrewise variance law holds for
arbitrary targets, but the arithmetic evaluation of $W$ and $T$ must be redone for a different one.

\subsection{Future directions}

An asymptotic evaluation of $W/T$ on $[a,a+w)$ would predict the numerical value $\approx0.05$, not
merely positivity and monotonicity; the natural route organises integers by squarefree kernel.
Sharper layer constants should follow from weighting by the actual prime distribution, giving a decay
ratio nearer $0.4$ than $0.5$ at $k=2$. The crude per-divisor discrepancy bound in
Theorem~\ref{thm:seed} could be improved by averaging over $d$. The fibrewise variance law applies to
any base statistic, yielding exact lift formulas for a whole family of feature comparisons. Finally,
analogues for higher moments of the residual would allow calibrated confidence statements from
arithmetic alone.

\section{Conclusion}

A small, stable, growing bump in explained variance turned out to be a piece of classical analytic
number theory in disguise. Splitting Chebyshev's identity $\sum_{d\mid n}\Lambda(d)=\log n$ into its
prime part and its higher-prime-power part splits the two competing features exactly: the base
feature is the former and the entire lift is the latter. From that identity flow an exact window law,
a linear density with constant $\sum_p\log p/(p(p-1))\approx0.7554$, an offset-free stability
estimate whose error is supported on the $O(\sqrt M\log M)$ higher prime powers, and --- most sharply
--- the fibrewise variance law $\Delta R^2=W/T$, which reduces a statistical measurement to a ratio of
two arithmetic sums of squares and delivers the clean dichotomy that the lift is positive precisely
when the window contains a radical collision with differing prime-power content.

